      ],
      "statement": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real Hilbert space with its Borel measurable structure, including rank zero. Let V:E→ℝ be C2, α>0, and D²V(x)[v,v]≥α‖v‖² for all x,v. Let η>0 and let f:E→ℝ be C1 with compact support. Define μ=volume.tilted(−V), R_y=μ.tilted(x↦−‖x−y‖²/(2η)), and the literal source law S_y=volume.tilted(u↦−V((y+u)/2)−‖y−u‖²/(8η)). Then for EVERY y, S_y=(x↦2x−y)_#R_y, and the exact mean function y↦∫f(u)S_y(du) is C1. All actual Gibbs normalization, integrability, affine law and mean regularity ingredients are produced internally. This is attributed analytic integration for PBPS C.1, with explicitly broader sufficient background assumptions, not a standalone printed main theorem or rough B.13.",
      "formula": "\\mu(dx)=Z_V^{-1}e^{-V(x)}dx,\\quad R_y(dx)=Z_R(y)^{-1}e^{-V(x)-\\|x-y\\|^2/(2\\eta)}dx,\\quad S_y(du)=Z_S(y)^{-1}e^{-V((y+u)/2)-\\|y-u\\|^2/(8\\eta)}du,\\quad S_y=(2x-y)_\\#R_y\\ (\\forall y),\\quad m(y)=\\int f\\,dS_y\\in C^1.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Produce genuine Gibbs normalization before using a tilt",
          "text": "Positive lower curvature and C2 V feed the existing Gibbs augmentation producer. Its actual positive partition integral is nonzero; Integrable.of_integral_ne_zero yields exp(−V)∈L1(volume). This establishes that the literal μ is a probability, rather than relying on a totalized zero integral. Finite-dimensional volume and its positive-open-set property are inherited ambient facts, not new theorem binders.",
          "formula": "Z_V=\\int e^{-V(x)}dx>0,\\qquad e^{-V}\\in L^1(dx),\\qquad\\mu=Z_V^{-1}e^{-V}dx,\\quad\\mu(E)=1.",
          "lean": "GibbsAugmentation.normalized_augmentation_density; hG; hI; Integrable.of_integral_ne_zero; isProbabilityMeasure_tilted"
        },
        {
          "title": "Identify the actual posterior as the combined normalized potential",
          "text": "Using the genuine exp(−V) integrability, the existing tilted_tilted theorem combines the literal μ tilt with its quadratic likelihood. Pointwise algebra gives the same combined potential V(x)+‖x−y‖²/(2η) at each y. No arbitrary almost-everywhere conditional representative is used.",
          "formula": "R_y=dx.\\operatorname{tilted}\\!\\left(-V(x)-\\frac{\\|x-y\\|^2}{2\\eta}\\right)\\qquad(\\forall y).",
          "lean": "hR; tilted_tilted hI; funext; Pi.add_apply; ring"
        },
        {
          "title": "Keep the exact inverse map and the factor8η",
          "text": "For T_y(x)=2x−y the inverse is (u+y)/2. Its displacement from y is (u−y)/2, so the original quadratic denominator2η becomes8η. This gives exactly the paper’s reflected source potential W_y. Its parameter-y score is −∇V((y+u)/2)/2−(y−u)/(4η); this differs from the u-gradient. The score formula is source context here, not an additional exported derivative theorem.",
          "formula": "T_y^{-1}(u)=\\frac{u+y}{2},\\quad T_y^{-1}(u)-y=\\frac{u-y}{2},\\quad W_y(u)=V\\!\\left(\\frac{y+u}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\|y-u\\|^2}{8\\eta}.",
          "lean": "hW; ha; hb; hq; norm_smul; norm_sub_rev; exact rw [hq,ha]"
        },
        {
          "title": "Apply the genuine normalized affine-map theorem",
          "text": "The existing affine Gibbs theorem applies to F_y(x)=V(x)+‖x−y‖²/(2η), scale2 and translation−y. Its change-of-variables normalization retains the inverse Jacobian2^(−d), equivalently Z_S(y)=2^d Z_R(y), so the normalized laws agree exactly. Substitute the proved inverse-potential identity and rewrite −y+2x=2x−y. The result is every-y S_y=(T_y)_#R_y, including d=0.",
          "formula": "d=\\dim E,\\quad |\\det DT_y^{-1}|=2^{-d},\\quad Z_S(y)=2^d Z_R(y),\\quad S_y=(T_y)_\\#R_y\\qquad(\\forall y).",
          "lean": "AffineGibbs.map_affine_gibbs; haf; hm; hR; hW; hS"
        },
        {
          "title": "Transport the whole literal mean function, then use true C1 regularity",
          "text": "The Gaussian reflected-mean parent applies to the actual probability μ, η>0 and compact C1 f. Its domination, numerator derivative, positive normalizer and continuous integrated derivative are internal verified background. Rewrite the measure equality at EVERY y in the whole mean function and consume this C1 result. An AE fiber identity would not justify this derivative transport.",
          "formula": "m(y)=\\int f(u)S_y(du)=\\int f(2x-y)R_y(dx)\\quad(\\forall y),\\qquad m\\in C^1(E).",
